            round_done: `Recommendation delivered`,
            r1_brief: `Monday 9 November 2026. Suministros Hosteleros Turia, in Paterna (Valencia), supplies consumables, cleaning products, packaging and small equipment to 640 bars, restaurants and hotels with 22 people. It sells through three routes, each with its own sales rep (Centro, Norte and Sur), and through an online shop opened in 2024. The manager, Vicente Llopis, calls in Nerea Soler, a junior sales technician since July: “For 2027 I have 40,000 euros for one thing only: a fourth rep to split the Norte route, investing in the online shop, or a minimum order and fortnightly delivery for small customers. On Friday at 12 I want your recommendation, with numbers.” He hands her his business-case template and the sales file from October 2025 to September 2026: 5,240 lines, the ERP export (routes and credit notes) with the shop export pasted underneath by her predecessor. Nerea has 4.5 hours a day for the assignment on top of her job: 9 hours over Monday and Tuesday. Opening the file she sees three things: March orders, from the week the ERP was migrated, that appear twice, some with the date written in another format; negative lines for returns, credit notes and shop refunds; and a shop export with amounts including VAT, test orders from the web agency, and a start date of 1 January. Today you decide how the file is cleaned.`,
            r2_brief: `Wednesday 11 November. With whatever clean data there are, it is time to measure. The manager’s template values each option for 2027 like this: the Norte rep sells 30% more of the route’s recurring sales, at its margin, and costs 40,000 euros a year; the online shop receives 25,000 euros of campaigns and catalogue that lift its sales by 45%, at its margin; the minimum order saves 110 euros of delivery a year for each small customer (under 1,500 euros of purchases a year) and costs 17,000 euros between the margin of the customers who will leave and the set-up. Today you decide which measure the channels are compared on, what is done with lines that have no cost price (mostly new products, many of them sold online) and how customers are counted: the same bar appears under several names, but every customer has a tax ID. You have 4.5 hours.`,
            r3_brief: `Thursday 12 November. Three things. In June a 58,000-euro order came in from a hotel opening in Peñíscola, on the Norte route: it will not recur. Over coffee the manager says: “It’s bound to be Norte; Tomás has been saying for twenty years that it’s big enough for two. And November and December in the shop were Black Friday and Christmas: that doesn’t count.” And Amparo, in administration, offers the accounting sales ledger by channel so Nerea can reconcile her figures, if she spends the time. You have 4.5 hours.`,
            r4_brief: `Friday 13 November, meeting at 12. Nerea has 3 hours to prepare what she will bring: what she recommends, in what format, and how she presents what she does not know for certain. The manager will put in place whatever she recommends and will ask her where it comes from. The result of this round is the 2027 value of that decision, calculated on the complete, clean data.`,
            brief_done: `The week is over. Review the recommendation, its value for 2027, the deviation of the data and the time used in the final summary.`,
            r1_heading: `Monday and Tuesday · Cleaning`,
